course: dictionary
lesson: 7691
title: Proud: which sense?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one you need. «She is proud of that word.» Here proud means pleased about something good she has done. You can check by swapping in pleased: "She is pleased of that word" sounds wrong, so try "She is pleased with that word" and it fits.
explain: «She is sad about the vase but proud of him for telling her.» The words sad and vase show that something went wrong, but proud of him for telling her shows the good sense: she admires him for being honest. Here proud is about someone close to you.
explain: Be careful with a bad sense. «A proud and rude king» means he thinks he is better than everyone else. The word rude is the clue. Pride can also mean respecting yourself so much that you won't ask for help, so read the words around it to find out which sense the writer means.
explain: Proud is a feeling word, and it is useful in healthy ways. Being proud of your effort helps you keep trying, and parents and teachers say «I'm proud of you» when you have worked hard. When you read it, ask: is this person pleased, or are they thinking they are better than others?
fact: Proud is an adjective: proud, prouder, proudest. The adverb is proudly, and the noun is pride.
fact: «Proud» can mean feeling pleased and happy about something good you or someone close to you has done.
fact: «Proud» can mean having respect for yourself, so you do not want to ask for help or seem weak.
fact: «Proud» can also mean thinking you are better than others, which is a bad sense, as in «a proud and rude king».
fact: Useful phrases: proud of, proud to, do someone proud, and a proud moment.
exercise: «She is proud of that word.» What does proud mean here? | *Pleased about something good she did | Cold and wet | Thinking she is better than everyone else | Too shy to speak
exercise: «She is sad about the vase but proud of him for telling her.» What does proud mean in this sentence? | Ashamed of the vase | *Pleased with him for being honest | Tired and sleepy | Angry with him for breaking things
exercise: The king would not listen to anyone, because he thought he was better than them. Which sense of proud is this? | *Thinking he is better than others, in a bad way | Proudly sewn on a flag | Pleased about a good deed | Having a proud moment at school
test: Which word is the noun form of proud? | *pride | proudly | proudness | prouder
test: Which word is the adverb of proud? | pride | prouder | proudest | *proudly
exam: Which sentence uses proud in the good sense of pleased? | A proud and rude prince shouted at the servants | *Ella felt proud of the poem she wrote for her gran | The king was too proud to listen to his cook | Proud people never ask anyone for anything
